1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Most agents don't have a tool problem. They have a context problem.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Before a task even begins, an agent can load dozens of tool schemas it may never use.
Forty tools, twelve thousand four hundred estimated context tokens — for a task that only needs a few.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Toolpath takes a different approach.
It models tools, capabilities, and entities as a graph, then resolves the path a task actually needs.
Traverse, don't preload.

4
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:41,000
A task maps to capabilities.
Graph traversal identifies the matching tools.
Only those tools need to enter context.
Three capabilities, three tools, one graph traversal, six hops.

5
00:00:41,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Let's run the demo.
The baseline preloads forty tools and estimates twelve thousand four hundred context tokens.
Toolpath identifies three relevant tools: repository resolution, pull request reading, and issue creation.
The graph makes that selection inspectable, and the execution log shows how the task progresses.

6
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:16,000
The result: one thousand eight hundred estimated context tokens instead of twelve thousand four hundred.
An estimated reduction of around eighty-five percent.

7
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The deployed demonstration uses a recorded snapshot, and the token figures are reproducible estimates, not live tokenizer measurements.
Less unnecessary context. More deliberate tool selection.

8
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Toolpath.
Traverse, don't preload.
